    "lawCode": "Add §170-e, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S427\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the executive law, in relation to directing the division\nof human rights to promulgate a statement regarding housing discrimi-\nnation that shall be appended to any form of housing assistance\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo notify housing assistance recipients of their rights to utilize their\nvouchers without discrimination and how they may make a complaint if\nthey believe they have been discriminated against.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the executive law by adding a new section 295-a that\ndirects the division of human rights to create and promulgate a state-\nment to be appended to any form of housing assistance administered by\nany state or municipal agency. The bill requires the statement to\ninclude certain information regarding \"source of income\" discrimination\nand to inform tenants of their right to use housing assistance and be\nfree from discriminatory or harassing terms, conditions or behavior. The\nbill also requires the statement to give tenants contact information\nthat would enable them to make a complaint if they feel that they are a\nvictim of source of income discrimination and to seek other assistance.\n\nSection 2 establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2019, New York passed legislation as part of the State Budget to\nprohibit housing discrimination based on lawful source of income, which\nprovided critical protections for low-income New Yorkers seeking stable\nhousing. We must take every possible step to inform recipients of hous-\ning assistance statewide that they are protected under law and connect\nthem with the means to make a complaint if they believe they have been\nthe victim of source of income discrimination.  Property owners who turn\naway housing assistance recipients exacerbate the problem of homeless-\nness in our state and undermine the programs that the state administers\nto provide stable and affordable housing to New Yorkers. With an\ninformed and empowered public, we can hold these bad actors accountable\nand ensure that New Yorkers are able to make use of programs to house\nthem.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nS.6713 of 2020 (Hoylman): Died in Rules\n\nA.11081 of 2020 (Epstein): Died in Governmental Operations\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n90 days after becoming law.\n",
